      "attachmentNote": "Homo sapiens, Gest (Anatomy Tables). Masseter from the zygomatic arch, temporalis from the temporal fossa, medial and lateral pterygoid from the pterygoid plates and the maxillary tuberosity, all to the mandible. **Four muscles, one nerve, and the nerve is the whole argument.** This record calls trigeminal innervation invariant and the muscle present wherever jaws are — the single most robust cranial homology in Gnathostomata. It also warns that subdivision patterns carry no homology weight on their own and the named parts are frequently non-homologous between clades, which is why the four human muscles are scored as parts of this record and **not** distributed among the `adductor-mandibulae-externus`, `-internus`, `-longus`, `-lateralis` and `-posterior` records. Those carve the sauropsid and anuran complex following Johnston's folded-sheet model, which is not a scheme a human jaw can be fitted into without inventing the fit.",
